(window.LADDER_CARDS["42a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42a1.1","q":"What is a graph, and how does it differ from the graph of 9e? What does the ends-count technique say, and why must the dots with an odd count of links be an even number?","a":"A set V of dots and a set E of links, each link a set of two different dots; in 9e a graph was the pairs of a function, drawn in the plane, and here nothing is drawn; adding the links at every dot counts every link exactly twice, Σ d(v) = 2|E|; the even counts add to an even number and the total is even, so the odd counts must be an even number of counts","w":["A drawing of dots and curves; it is the same as 9e’s graph; the ends-count technique says every dot has two links; the odd dots are even in number because links come in pairs","A set of dots and links; it differs from 9e only in name; adding the links at every dot counts every link once; the number of odd dots is odd","A set of dots and a set of links; in 9e a graph was a set of dots too; the technique says Σ d(v) = |E|²; the odd dots are even because V is finite"],"why":"One set of ends, counted two ways: by dot and by link."},
 {"u":"42a2.1","q":"What is the difference between a walk, a path and a cycle? What does the walk-to-path technique say, and which line of its proof uses the least walk?","a":"A walk is a list of dots each joined to the next; a path is a walk that meets no dot twice; a cycle is a walk of length at least 3 that ends where it starts and meets no other dot twice; if a walk joins u to w, a path joins u to w: take a walk of least length, and if it met a dot twice, cutting out the stretch between would give a shorter walk, against least","w":["A walk meets no dot twice, a path may, and a cycle is any closed path; every path is a walk of least length; the least walk is used in the first line only","A walk is a list of links, a path a list of dots, and a cycle a walk of length 2; if a path joins u to w then a cycle does; no line uses the least walk","A walk is a list of dots each joined to the next; a path is a walk that ends where it starts; every walk is a path; the least walk is used to count the links"],"why":"Being joined by a walk is a sameness relation, and its classes are the pieces."},
 {"u":"42a3.1","q":"What is a two-sided graph? What does the odd-cycle technique say, and how does its second proof choose the two sides?","a":"V splits into two sets X and Y, with no dot in both, so that every link has one dot in each; a graph is two-sided exactly when it has no cycle of odd length; in each piece take a dot r, and put v in X when its distance from r is even and in Y when it is odd","w":["Every dot has two links; a graph is two-sided exactly when it has no cycle; the sides are chosen by a coin","V splits into X and Y with every link inside one side; a graph is two-sided exactly when every cycle is odd; the sides are the dots with even and with odd counts of links","V splits into X and Y with every link crossing; a graph is two-sided exactly when it has no triangle; the sides are chosen by trying all 2ⁿ splits"],"why":"A link inside one side would close a walk of odd length, which holds an odd cycle."},
 {"u":"42a4.1","q":"Why are there 2^(n(n − 1)/2) graphs on n named dots? Why is the number of shapes of graph on four dots smaller than 64?","a":"There are n(n − 1)/2 possible links, by choosing-counts-parts (4k), and a graph is a part of them, so settled-parts (4k) gives 2 to that power; many of the 64 graphs on four named dots are the same shape with only the names moved, as the six graphs with one link are one shape: there are 11 shapes","w":["Each dot is joined or not, giving 2ⁿ graphs; the shapes are fewer because some graphs have no links","There are n² possible links; the number of shapes is 64, the same as the number of graphs","Each link has n choices of dot; the shapes are fewer because drawings can be turned over"],"why":"n = 4 gives 2⁶ = 64, and n = 10 gives 2⁴⁵."},
 {"u":"42a5.1","q":"Why can no graph have links counts 3, 3, 2, 1? Why does having no triangle not make a graph two-sided?","a":"They add to 9, which is odd, and the ends-count technique needs an even total; and a cycle of 5 links is odd and has no triangle","w":["Four dots cannot have a count of 3; and a graph with no triangle has no cycle at all","The counts must all be equal; and a triangle is the only odd cycle","A count of 1 is not allowed; and two-sided graphs are those with a triangle"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42b1.1","q":"Why are the two ends of a longest path in a tree ends of the tree? What does the links-of-a-tree technique say, and why does taking an end away keep a tree?","a":"A neighbour of v₀ other than v₁ would lie on the path, closing a cycle, or off it, making a longer path, so v₀ has one link, and so has vₖ; a tree with n dots has n − 1 links; taking an end v away makes no cycle, and the rest stays in one piece, since a path through v would need two links at v","w":["The ends of a longest path have the most links; a tree with n dots has n links; taking an end away may split the tree","A longest path uses every dot; a tree with n dots has n − 1 links; taking any dot away keeps a tree","The ends of any path are ends of the tree; a tree with n dots has 2n − 2 links; taking an end away adds a cycle"],"why":"Induction on n: the end took one link with it."},
 {"u":"42b2.1","q":"What does the any-two-of-three technique say? Why does dropping a link that lies on a cycle keep the graph in one piece?","a":"For a graph with n dots, any two of these give the third: it is in one piece; it has no cycle; it has n − 1 links; a walk that used the dropped link goes round the rest of the cycle instead","w":["A graph with n dots has all three or none; dropping a link of a cycle always splits the graph","Any one of the three gives the other two; dropping a link keeps one piece because n − 1 links are left","One piece and a cycle give n − 1 links; dropping a link of a cycle keeps one piece because the cycle is odd"],"why":"A graph is a tree exactly when each two of its dots are joined by exactly one path."},
 {"u":"42b3.1","q":"What is a spanning tree? Why does the recipe of the spanning-tree technique always stop, and why is the graph still in one piece when it does?","a":"A tree whose dots are all the dots of G and whose links are links of G; while H has a cycle the recipe drops one link of it, so the number of links falls at every move and every run stops (4l); each drop keeps one piece, by 42b2, and at the stop there is no cycle","w":["A tree with the fewest dots of G; the recipe stops because G is finite in dots; it is in one piece because it has n links","A path through every dot; the recipe adds links until a cycle appears; it stops when the graph splits","A tree on the dots of G; the recipe stops because every graph has one cycle; one piece is kept only when the cheapest link is dropped"],"why":"Which links to drop is free, and the trees that come out differ."},
 {"u":"42b4.1","q":"How is the leaf code of a tree made? Why does a dot appear in the code one time fewer than its count of links, and how does that let the code be read back?","a":"Take away the end with the smallest name, write down the name of its one neighbour, and repeat until two dots are left: n − 2 numbers; a dot is written once for each neighbour taken away before it, and ends never appear; so the first end taken is the smallest name not in the code: join it to the code’s first entry, strike both and go on, joining the last two dots","w":["Write the names of the ends in order; each dot appears once for each link; the code is read back by sorting it","Take away the end with the largest name and write its own name; a dot appears as often as it has links; the code cannot be read back","Take away the smallest end and write its neighbour; a dot appears one time more than its count of links; the code is read back from its last entry"],"why":"A matching between the trees on n named dots and the nⁿ⁻² lists."},
 {"u":"42b5.1","q":"Why is a graph with n dots and n − 1 links not always a tree? Why does nⁿ⁻² not count the shapes of tree?","a":"A triangle and a lone dot have 4 dots and 3 links: it needs one piece too; and nⁿ⁻² counts trees on named dots: on four dots there are 16 trees and 2 shapes","w":["It is always a tree; and nⁿ⁻² counts shapes, since names do not matter","It may have too many ends; and nⁿ⁻² counts only the paths","A tree needs n links; and nⁿ⁻² counts the shapes twice each"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42c1.1","q":"Why does a tour of the links use the links at a passing dot in pairs? Why do four dots with odd counts rule out a tour of the seven bridges?","a":"A tour enters and leaves each dot it passes through by two different links, so a dot that is neither the start nor the end has an even count; the four parts have counts 5, 3, 3, 3, all odd, and at most two dots can be the start and the end","w":["A tour uses each link twice; four odd dots rule out a tour because 4 is even","A tour may enter a dot without leaving; the bridges fail because there are seven of them","A tour uses the links at a dot in pairs because links are sets of two dots; four odd counts rule out only a closed tour"],"why":"Counting says no to Königsberg."},
 {"u":"42c2.1","q":"What does the even-tour technique say? Why can the walk in its proof stop only at the dot it started from?","a":"A graph in one piece, with at least one link, has a closed tour of the links exactly when every dot has an even count of links; entering any other dot uses one link of an even count, so one is free to leave by","w":["A graph has a closed tour exactly when it has an even number of links; the walk stops at the start because it is the nearest dot","A graph in one piece has a closed tour exactly when every count is odd; the walk can stop anywhere","A graph with every count even has a closed tour, in one piece or not; the walk stops at the start because links are finite"],"why":"The pieces of what is left have tours by induction, spliced into the closed walk."},
 {"u":"42c3.1","q":"What is a round of the dots, and how does the question of one differ from the question of a tour of the links? What does the half-the-dots technique say, and where does its proof use the pigeonhole technique?","a":"A cycle through every dot exactly once; the tour question is settled by a count at each dot, and for the round no such count is known: deciding it is among the hardest questions of 41h’s family; a graph with n ≥ 3 dots, each with links to at least n/2 dots, has a round of the dots; pigeonhole gives two unjoined dots a shared neighbour, and makes the two sets of places along a longest path too big to miss each other","w":["A walk using every link once; the two questions are the same; every graph with n/2 links has a round; pigeonhole counts the links","A cycle through every dot once; it is settled by even counts; a graph with every count at least 2 has a round; pigeonhole is not used","A cycle through every dot once; no count is known to settle it; a graph with counts at least (n − 1)/2 has a round; pigeonhole picks the longest path"],"why":"Two copies of Kₘ sharing one dot show n/2 cannot be lowered."},
 {"u":"42c4.1","q":"Why do even counts not give a closed tour in a graph that is not in one piece? Why does a closed tour of the links not have to be a round of the dots?","a":"Two separate triangles have even counts and no tour, since a walk cannot cross between pieces; and a tour may pass a dot many times: it is the links that are used once","w":["Even counts always give a closed tour; and a closed tour passes every dot exactly once","Two pieces have odd counts; and a tour misses the dots with two links","A graph in two pieces has no links; and a tour is a round whenever the counts are even"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42d1.1","q":"What is a flat graph, and what are its faces? How does the flat-count technique follow from 34f, and why do all flat drawings of one graph have the same number of faces?","a":"A graph that can be drawn on a flat sheet with each link a curve between its two dots and no two curves meeting except at a shared dot; the faces are the parts the curves cut the sheet into, the outside among them; wrapping the sheet round a sphere and closing it at a point of the outside face gives a drawing on the sphere, where 34f gives V − E + F = 2; so F = 2 − V + E, fixed by the graph","w":["A graph with no cycle; its faces are its links; the flat-count follows from 42a’s ends-count; drawings differ in their number of faces","A graph drawn with straight links; its faces are its triangles; 34f gives V + E + F = 2; the faces agree because every face has three links","A graph that can be drawn without crossings; the faces do not count the outside; 34f gives V − E + F = 1; the count differs between drawings by one"],"why":"Held in trust, as on rung 34: a closed curve that does not cross itself splits the sheet into two parts."},
 {"u":"42d2.1","q":"What does the flat-ceiling technique say? Why does K₃,₃ need the bound for graphs with no triangle, when K₅ needs only the first one?","a":"A flat graph with V ≥ 3 dots has at most 3V − 6 links, and at most 2V − 4 if it has no triangle; K₅ has 10 links, above 3 · 5 − 6 = 9; K₃,₃ has 9 links, below 3 · 6 − 6 = 12, but it is two-sided, so it has no triangle, and 9 is above 2 · 6 − 4 = 8","w":["A flat graph has at least 3V − 6 links; K₃,₃ has too few links to be flat; K₅ has too few as well","A flat graph has at most 3V − 6 links; K₅ has 9 links and K₃,₃ has 12, so only K₃,₃ breaks the first bound","A flat graph has at most 2V links; K₃,₃ needs the second bound because it has triangles; K₅ breaks neither"],"why":"Each rim uses at least 3 links and each link has two sides: 3F ≤ 2E."},
 {"u":"42d3.1","q":"What does the five-or-fewer technique say? Which two earlier techniques does its proof add together?","a":"Every flat graph has a dot with at most five links; the ends-count technique gives Σ d(v) = 2E, and the flat-ceiling technique gives 2E ≤ 6V − 12, so not every d(v) can be 6 or more","w":["Every dot of a flat graph has at most five links; it uses the flat-count and odd-cycle techniques","Every flat graph has a dot with exactly five links; it uses the tree-ends and links-of-a-tree techniques","Every flat graph has at most five dots; it uses the ends-count technique twice"],"why":"The average count is below 6."},
 {"u":"42d4.1","q":"What is a two-colour swap, and why does it leave a colouring? In the five-colour technique, why can the piece of colours 2 and 4 that holds v₂ not reach v₄?","a":"Swapping two colours in one piece of the graph of the dots of those two colours; inside the piece the colours trade places, and a link out of the piece goes to a dot of another colour; a path from v₁ to v₃ in colours 1 and 3, with v, closes a cycle with v₂ inside and v₄ outside, and to cross it the piece would meet a dot of colour 1 or 3, or v","w":["Swapping the colours of two joined dots; it leaves a colouring because they differed; the 2 and 4 piece cannot reach v₄ because v₄ is coloured 5","Swapping two colours everywhere; it changes nothing; the 2 and 4 piece does reach v₄, and the swap is made anyway","Swapping two colours in one piece of the two-colour graph; it may break the colouring at the rim; the piece cannot reach v₄ because flat graphs have no long paths"],"why":"With four colours the same move fails: one swap can undo the other."},
 {"u":"42d5.1","q":"Why does E ≤ 3V − 6 not make a graph flat? Why is 3F = E wrong?","a":"K₃,₃ has 9 ≤ 12 links and is not flat: the ceiling is needed, not enough; and each link rims two faces, so the count is 3F ≤ 2E","w":["E ≤ 3V − 6 does make a graph flat; and 3F = E is right for triangles","K₅ has 9 links and is flat; and 3F = E fails because faces have four links","The bound is only for trees; and 3F = E counts the outside face twice"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42e1.1","q":"What is a matching in a graph, and how is a matching on a two-sided graph a matching in the sense of 9c? Why does a matching covering X force |N(S)| ≥ |S| for every part S of X?","a":"A set M of links, no two of which share a dot; on a two-sided graph it is exactly a one-to-one function from the matched dots of X onto the matched dots of Y that sends each dot along a link; if a matching covers X, the partners of S are |S| different dots of N(S)","w":["A set of dots, no two joined; it is a 9c matching because the dots are paired by name; |N(S)| ≥ |S| holds because every dot has a neighbour","A set of links that covers every dot; it is no 9c matching, since links are not functions; |N(S)| ≥ |S| is forced only for single dots","A set of links, no two sharing a dot; it is a 9c matching from all of X onto all of Y always; |N(S)| ≥ |S| because N(S) holds S"],"why":"A pick from every box N({x}), with no two picks the same."},
 {"u":"42e2.1","q":"What does the marriage technique say? In case two, why does every T outside S₀ still have enough neighbours outside N(S₀)?","a":"On a two-sided graph with sides X and Y, X finite, some matching covers X exactly when every part S of X has |N(S)| ≥ |S|; the condition on T ∪ S₀ gives |N(T) − N(S₀)| = |N(T ∪ S₀)| − |N(S₀)| ≥ |T| + |S₀| − |S₀| = |T|, since S₀ is tight","w":["Some matching covers X exactly when |Y| ≥ |X|; T has enough neighbours because Y is larger than X","Some matching covers X exactly when every dot of X has a neighbour; T has enough because S₀ used none of Y","Some matching covers X exactly when every S has |N(S)| ≥ |S|; T has enough because N(T) never meets N(S₀)"],"why":"A no comes with a certificate: one group S with too few neighbours."},
 {"u":"42e3.1","q":"What is a growing path, and how does swapping along one make the matching larger? Why does a larger matching M′ give a growing path for M?","a":"An alternating path whose two ends are both unmatched; it has k links in M and k + 1 out, so swapping in for out gives a matching with one more link; in the links in exactly one of M and M′ every dot has at most two, so the pieces are alternating paths and even cycles, and some piece has more links of M′ than of M: a path that starts and ends in M′","w":["A path all of whose links are in M; swapping removes it; a larger M′ gives a growing path because M′ holds M","An alternating path with both ends matched; swapping keeps the size; a larger M′ gives no growing path","An alternating path with unmatched ends; swapping adds two links; M′ gives a growing path because the even cycles have more links of M′"],"why":"So a matching is largest exactly when it has no growing path."},
 {"u":"42e4.1","q":"What is a cover, and why is every cover at least as large as every matching? What does the cover-equals-matching technique say, and why does it fail on a triangle?","a":"A set K of dots that holds at least one dot of every link; each link of a matching needs its own guard, since no dot holds two of them; on a two-sided graph the most links in a matching equals the fewest dots in a cover; on a triangle the largest matching has one link and the smallest cover two dots: the triangle is not two-sided","w":["A set of links holding every dot; a cover is smaller than a matching; the technique holds on every graph, the triangle included","A set of dots holding a dot of every link; every cover equals every matching in size; it fails on a triangle because a triangle has no matching","A set of dots, no two joined; covers and matchings are not comparable; the triangle has a matching of 2 and a cover of 1"],"why":"42f finds the same equality as a case of flows, and 42g as a case of prices."},
 {"u":"42e5.1","q":"Why is a matching that cannot be added to not always largest? Why is checking |N(S)| ≥ |S| for single dots not enough?","a":"On the path of three links, the middle link alone blocks both others, and the two end links are larger; and two nurses who can both work only the same one shift have one neighbour each and one between them: the condition is on every part S","w":["It is always largest; and single dots are enough when every dot has a neighbour","It may hold a cycle; and single dots fail because N(S) may be empty for a pair","A matching can always be added to; and the condition is only on X itself"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42f1.1","q":"What is a flow, and what is a cut? What does the flow-under-cut technique say, and why does every cut carry the same net flow?","a":"A flow gives each one-way link a number between 0 and its capacity so that at every dot other than s and t what flows in equals what flows out; a cut is a set S of dots holding s and not t, with capacity the total of the links from S to the rest; the value of any flow is at most the capacity of any cut; summing out less in over the dots of S, only s gives a nonzero term and links inside S cancel, so the net flow across S is the value","w":["A flow is a path from s to t; a cut is a link whose loss parts them; every flow equals every cut; the net flow differs from cut to cut","A flow gives each link a number up to its capacity with no balance asked; a cut is a set holding s and t; the value is at least every cut","A flow balances at every dot, s and t too; a cut is a set of links; the flow-under-cut technique holds only for the smallest cut"],"why":"The ledger kept at each dot is kept across every line drawn between s and t."},
 {"u":"42f2.1","q":"What are room forward and room back? What does the push-along technique say, and why is the balance kept at a dot where the route steps back against a link?","a":"Room forward is c(u, w) − f(u, w), and room back is f(u, w), since sending less along a link is the same as sending some back; if a route with room has least room r, pushing r along it gives a flow of value |f| + r; forward steps gain r and backward steps lose r, and at each inside dot one step comes in and one goes out, so in each of the four cases in and out change alike","w":["Room forward is f and room back is c; pushing r gives a flow of value r; the balance is broken at a backward step and mended later","Room forward is c − f and there is no room back; pushing along a route needs every link empty; backward steps are not allowed","Room forward is c − f and room back is f; pushing the greatest room gives a flow; the balance is kept because r is 0 at backward steps"],"why":"In the worked network three pushes give 14, the capacity of the cut {s, p, q}."},
 {"u":"42f3.1","q":"What does the bottleneck technique say? Why is every link from S to the rest full, and every link from the rest into S empty?","a":"If no route has room, the dots reachable from s by steps with room form a cut whose capacity is the value of the flow, so the largest flow equals the smallest cut; room forward on a link from S would put its far dot in S, and room back on a link into S would put its near dot in S","w":["If no route has room the flow is 0; links from S are full because capacities are whole; links into S are empty by choice","The largest flow is below the smallest cut; links from S are empty and links into S full","If no route has room, S holds t; every link is full, in both directions"],"why":"|f| = c(S) − 0, at least every flow and at most every cut."},
 {"u":"42f4.1","q":"How is a two-sided graph made into a network whose largest flow is its largest matching? Why does that use the whole-flow technique?","a":"Add a source joined to each dot of X and a sink joined from each dot of Y, turn each link into a one-way link from X to Y, and give every link capacity 1; a matching of k links gives a flow of value k, and a whole flow of value k gives a matching, since each x sends 1 to at most one y; the whole-flow technique gives a largest flow that is whole","w":["Give each link its own source; the largest flow is the number of links; whole flows are not needed","Join s to Y and t to X with capacity |X|; a flow of value k gives a cover; the whole-flow technique makes the flow stop","Add a source and a sink with capacity 1 links; any flow of value k is a matching; the whole-flow technique is used to find the cut"],"why":"So the cover-equals-matching technique is the bottleneck technique on a special network."},
 {"u":"42f5.1","q":"Why must a route with room be allowed to step back against a link? Why does a cut’s capacity count only the links from S to the rest?","a":"With no path having room forward the flow may still not be largest: a route may need a step back against a used link, and room back counts; and the links back into S do not bound the flow out of S, carrying nothing in a largest flow","w":["Steps back are never needed; and a cut counts links both ways across","A step back empties the network; and the capacity counts only links into S","Routes must alternate forward and back; and a cut counts the dots of S"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42g1.1","q":"What is a corner of the allowed plans in n unknowns? What does the tight-corner technique say, and why does each move of its proof add a row that is free from the old ones?","a":"An allowed plan at which some n of the m + n limits hold with equality and have rows that make a free list (27b); if the allowed plans have a corner and c · x is bounded above on them, some corner gives the largest value; the move follows a direction d at right angles to every old tight row until a new limit is tight, and d is not at right angles to the new row","w":["Any allowed plan; every corner gives the largest value; each move adds a row by choosing the nearest limit","A plan at which all m + n limits hold with equality; the best plan is never a corner; the new row is free because d is 0","An allowed plan with n tight limits; some corner is best only when the region is bounded; the new row is free because limits are never parallel"],"why":"After at most n moves a corner at least as good; the corners are finitely many."},
 {"u":"42g2.1","q":"What are covering prices, and what is the twin program? What does the price-bound technique say, and which two facts about signs does its proof need?","a":"Prices y ≥ 0 on the limits with Aᵀy ≥ c: the hours each unit uses are worth at least what it earns; the twin program asks for the least b · y over such y; if x is an allowed plan and y covering prices, then c · x ≤ b · y; the proof needs x ≥ 0, to go from c ≤ Aᵀy to c · x ≤ (Aᵀy) · x, and y ≥ 0, to go from Ax ≤ b to y · (Ax) ≤ y · b","w":["Prices with Aᵀy ≤ c; the twin asks for the largest b · y; c · x ≥ b · y; the proof needs A ≥ 0","Prices on the unknowns; the twin is the same program; c · x = b · y for every pair; no signs are needed","Prices y ≥ 0 with Aᵀy ≥ c; the twin asks for the least b · y; c · x ≤ b · y; the proof needs b ≥ 0 and c ≥ 0"],"why":"y = (0, 3/2, 1) totals 36, and the plan (2, 6) earns 36: both are best."},
 {"u":"42g3.1","q":"What does the one-out technique do to a list of limits? What does the no-answer technique say, and why is its certificate easy to check?","a":"It sorts the limits by the sign of xₙ’s coefficient and replaces them by the zero ones together with one limit for each pair of an upper and a lower one, a sum with weights at least 0 in which xₙ drops out; the new list has an answer exactly when the old one has; Ax ≤ b has no answer exactly when some y ≥ 0 has yᵀA = 0 and y · b < 0; checking y is a few sums","w":["It drops the limit with the largest bound; Ax ≤ b has no answer exactly when b has a negative entry; the certificate is the list itself","It solves for xₙ and puts it back; Ax ≤ b always has an answer; no certificate is needed","It takes one limit out; no answer exactly when some y has yᵀA = b; the certificate is checked by trying every x"],"why":"A no always comes with a short reason, as the marriage technique’s did."},
 {"u":"42g4.1","q":"What does the equal-prices technique say? Which list of limits does its proof show has an answer, and why does that answer give equal values?","a":"If the program has an allowed plan and its twin has covering prices, both have best answers, and the best plan earns exactly the least price total; the list asks for x allowed, y covering and c · x ≥ b · y together; the price-bound technique gives c · x ≤ b · y too, so the values are equal and both are best","w":["A best plan earns less than the least price total; the list asks for x alone; the values differ by the slack","If either program has an answer both do; the list asks for c · x ≤ b · y; the values are equal by choice of units","The best plan earns the least price total only at a corner; the list has no answer; the values are equal by the no-answer technique"],"why":"Two questions, one about making and one about pricing, have one answer."},
 {"u":"42g5.1","q":"Why is the best plan not always only a corner? Why does a total of covering prices not give a plan that earns it?","a":"Some corner is best, but a whole edge between two best corners may be best too; and prices bound plans from above: the least total equals the best plan, not every total","w":["The best plan is never a corner; and every total of covering prices is earned by some plan","Corners are not allowed plans; and prices bound plans from below","The best plan is the middle of the region; and a total of prices is earned by doubling the plan"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42h1.1","q":"What is a mix, and what is the average payment when both players mix? What does the guarantee-order technique say, and why can the column player answer a fixed mix with one column?","a":"A mix is a list of chances xᵢ ≥ 0 adding to 1 over a player’s choices; with independent mixes x and y the average payment is xᵀMy; what the row player can guarantee is at most what the column player can hold it to: the largest over x of the least over y is at most the least over y of the largest over x; against a fixed x, xᵀMy is a mix of the column averages, so it is least at the least of them","w":["A mix is one fixed choice; the average payment is the largest entry; the guarantee-order technique says the two guarantees are equal; a single column is best because columns are longer","A mix is a list of chances adding to 1; the average is xᵀMy; the row player’s guarantee is at least the column player’s hold; a single column answers because the row player moves first","A mix is a list of payments; the average is the sum of the grid; the technique orders the rows by gain; the column player must always mix"],"why":"A least is at most any value, and a largest at least."},
 {"u":"42h2.1","q":"What does the minimax technique say? Which two programs does its proof write, and why are they twins?","a":"In every game of gain against loss the largest over x of the least over y of xᵀMy equals the least over y of the largest over x, the value of the game; with all entries made positive, the row player’s best guarantee becomes the least Σ uᵢ with Mᵀu ≥ 1, u ≥ 0, and the column player’s the largest Σ zⱼ with Mz ≤ 1, z ≥ 0: a program of 42g with A = M, b = 1, c = 1 and its twin, so the equal-prices technique gives 1/v = 1/w","w":["The two guarantees differ by the largest entry; the proof writes one program for both players; no twin is needed","The value of every game is 0; the programs are the row and column sums; they are twins because the grid is square","The guarantees are equal only for fixed choices; the programs are Mx ≤ 0 and My ≥ 0; they are twins by the price-bound technique alone"],"why":"A player who announces a best mix in advance loses nothing by it."},
 {"u":"42h3.1","q":"What does the indifference technique say, and why does it need the game to have no safe choice? How are the taker’s chance x and the value found in the penalty game?","a":"In a two-by-two game with no safe choice, each player’s best mix makes the other player’s two choices earn the same; with no safe choice each best mix uses both choices, and if the other’s two choices earned differently the other would use only the better; 0.58x + 0.93(1 − x) = 0.95x + 0.70(1 − x) gives 0.23 = 0.60x, x ≈ 0.383, and the value is about 0.93 − 0.35 · 0.383 ≈ 0.796","w":["Each player’s best mix is one half on each choice; it needs no condition; x = 0.5 and the value is the average of the four entries, 0.79","Each best mix makes the player’s own two choices earn the same; it needs a safe choice; x is the largest entry, 0.95","Each best mix makes the other’s choices earn the same; x solves 0.58x = 0.95(1 − x), about 0.62; the value is 0.58"],"why":"The value is the same from either side, by the minimax technique."},
 {"u":"42h4.1","q":"What is an equilibrium of a game that is not gain against loss? Why is “both confess” an equilibrium, though both would rather both stay silent?","a":"A pair of mixes such that neither player can do better by changing their own mix alone; whatever the other does, confessing is shorter, 0 years against 1 or 5 against 10, so neither gains by changing alone, though both silent would cost each 1 year in place of 5","w":["A pair of choices best for both players; both confess is one because 5 years is the shortest term","A pair of mixes that pays both players the same; both confess is no equilibrium, since both silent is better","A pair of mixes the players agree on beforehand; both confess is an equilibrium because the accused cannot speak"],"why":"Stated, from 1950: every finite game has an equilibrium in mixes."},
 {"u":"42h5.1","q":"Why does a best mix not make every choice equally likely? Why does the minimax technique fail for fixed choices?","a":"A best mix gives the other player’s choices the same earnings, not the same chances: 0.383 is not one half; and in stone, paper, scissors fixed rows guarantee −1 while fixed columns hold to 1","w":["A best mix is always one half each; and the minimax technique holds for fixed choices too","A best mix favours the larger entry; and fixed choices fail because the grid is square","A best mix copies the other player; and fixed choices give the value 0"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42i1.1","q":"What does the crowded-box technique say, and how is the pigeonhole technique a case of it? In the rise-or-fall technique, why must two places with the same label hold falling numbers?","a":"If more than n · k things go into n boxes, some box gets more than k; with k = 1 it is the pigeonhole technique; the label of a place is the length of the longest rising run starting there, and if an earlier place held a smaller number than a later one, its run could go on through the later one and its label would be larger; so with equal labels the earlier number is the larger","w":["If n · k things go into n boxes, every box gets k; pigeonhole is the case n = 1; equal labels mean equal numbers","If more than n · k things go into n boxes, some box gets more than k; pigeonhole is the case k = 0; equal labels hold rising numbers","If more than n things go into k boxes, some box is empty; pigeonhole is the same statement; the labels fall because the numbers are sorted"],"why":"n² + 1 > n · n, so n + 1 places share a label."},
 {"u":"42i2.1","q":"What does the over-and-under technique say? Why is a member lying in exactly r of the sets counted 0 times in all, and which technique of rung 4 shows it?","a":"The number of members of U lying in none of A₁, …, Aₘ is |U| minus the sizes of the Aᵢ, plus the sizes of the overlaps of two, minus those of three, and so on; a member in exactly r of the sets lies in the overlaps of every j of its r sets, so it is counted 1 − C(r, 1) + C(r, 2) − ⋯ ± C(r, r) = (1 − 1)ʳ = 0 times, by the two-term-power technique (4k)","w":["The number in none of the sets is |U| minus the sizes of the Aᵢ; a member in r sets is counted −r times; 4h shows it","The number in none is the sum of the overlaps; a member in r sets is counted once; choosing-counts-parts alone shows it","The number in all of the sets is |U| minus the overlaps; a member in r sets is counted 2ʳ times; 4c shows it"],"why":"For 1 to 100 and 2, 3, 5: 100 − 103 + 32 − 3 = 26."},
 {"u":"42i3.1","q":"What does the wrong-coats technique say? Why is Dₙ within 1/(n + 1) of n!/e?","a":"Dₙ = n!(1 − 1/1! + 1/2! − ⋯ + (−1)ⁿ/n!), and for n ≥ 1 it is the whole number nearest n!/e; e⁻¹ is the same sum carried on for ever (22j), so n!/e − Dₙ is n! times the terms past n, which alternate and shrink, and their sum is smaller than the first, n!/(n + 1)! = 1/(n + 1)","w":["Dₙ = n!/2; it is within 1/(n + 1) of n!/e because e is near 2","Dₙ = n! − (n − 1)!; the gap to n!/e grows with n","Dₙ = n!(1 + 1/1! + ⋯ + 1/n!); it is near n!/e because the sum is e"],"why":"The chance that nobody gets their own coat heads to 1/e ≈ 0.3679."},
 {"u":"42i4.1","q":"Why does the crowded-box technique give at least five people, and not exactly five? Why is 100 − (50 + 33 + 20) not the count?","a":"The technique gives a floor, not a count: some box has more than 4, and it may have many more; and the multiples of 6, 10 and 15 were struck twice, so they are added back, and the multiples of 30 taken off again","w":["Every box has exactly five; and 100 − 103 is the count, since it is negative only by rounding","The technique gives a ceiling of five; and the multiples of 30 were never struck","Five is the average; and the count is 100 − 50 − 33"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42j1.1","q":"What does the six-guests technique say? Why must three of the five links at a dot of K₆ share a colour, and how does K₅ escape?","a":"Every red and blue colouring of the links of K₆ has a one-colour triangle, and some colouring of K₅ has none; 5 > 2 · 2, so by the crowded-box technique one colour has three of the five links; in K₅ colour the ring of five red and the five crossing links blue: the ring and the star of five each have no triangle","w":["Every colouring of K₅ has a one-colour triangle; three links share a colour because 5 is odd; K₄ escapes","Some colouring of K₆ has a one-colour triangle; three links share a colour by choice; K₅ escapes by using three colours","Every colouring of K₆ has a one-colour triangle; all five links at a dot share a colour; K₅ escapes because it has fewer than six links"],"why":"R(3, 3) = 6."},
 {"u":"42j2.1","q":"What is the party number R(s, t), and what does the party-number technique say? Why do the n − 1 links at v give R(s − 1, t) red or R(s, t − 1) blue?","a":"The least n such that every colouring of Kₙ has s dots with every link between them red, or t with every link blue; for all s, t ≥ 2 it exists, and R(s, t) ≤ R(s − 1, t) + R(s, t − 1); with n the sum of the two, fewer of each would give n − 1 ≤ R(s − 1, t) − 1 + R(s, t − 1) − 1, one too few","w":["The largest n with a colouring that has no one-colour group; R(s, t) = R(s − 1, t) + R(s, t − 1) exactly; the links split evenly","The least n such that some colouring has s red or t blue; R(s, t) ≤ R(s − 1, t − 1); the links at v are all one colour","The least n such that every colouring has s red and t blue; R(s, t) ≥ R(s − 1, t) + R(s, t − 1); half the links are red"],"why":"So R(s, t) is at most C(s + t − 2, s − 1), by 4k’s row-step."},
 {"u":"42j3.1","q":"What does the no-triangle technique say? Why is d(u) + d(w) at most n for a link {u, w} of a graph with no triangle?","a":"A graph on n dots with no triangle has at most n²/4 links; u and w share no neighbour, since a shared one would close a triangle, so their neighbours are separate sets among the n dots","w":["A graph with no triangle has at most n links; d(u) + d(w) ≤ n because each dot has at most n/2 links","A graph on n dots with n²/4 links has a triangle; d(u) + d(w) ≤ n because u and w are not joined","A graph with no triangle has at most n²/2 links; d(u) + d(w) ≤ n by the ends-count technique"],"why":"The equal two-sided graph has exactly n²/4 links and no triangle."},
 {"u":"42j4.1","q":"What does the average-below-one technique say, and which fact about averages proves it? Why is the chance that a given group of k dots is one colour 2 times 2 to the minus k choose 2?","a":"If the average number of one-colour k-groups in a random colouring of Kₙ is below 1, some colouring of Kₙ has none, and R(k, k) > n; a count that is always at least 1 has average at least 1; the group has C(k, 2) links, each coloured by an independent fair coin, and they are all red or all blue","w":["If the average is below 1, most colourings have no one-colour group; an average is the most likely value; the chance is 2^(−k), one coin for each dot","If the average is above 1, every colouring has a one-colour group; averages add; the chance is 1/k","If the average is below 1, some colouring has none; a count below 1 is 0; the chance is 2^(−C(k, 2)), since only red counts"],"why":"Some colouring of K₁₀₀ has no one-colour group of ten, and none has ever been written down."},
 {"u":"42j5.1","q":"Why does an average below one not show that most colourings have no one-colour group? Why does an average above one not show that every colouring has one?","a":"An average below one gives some colouring with none; how many have none needs more than the average; and an average can be large because a few colourings have many","w":["An average of 0.3 means 70% have none; and an average above one means every colouring has at least one","An average below one means none have any; and an average above one means half have one","Averages say nothing at all about colourings; and an average above one is not possible"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42k1.1","q":"What does the layer-by-layer technique say? Why is its cost |V| plus 2|E|, even when one dot has very many links?","a":"Walking out from r with a queue, taking out the first dot and putting in each unseen neighbour at its distance plus 1, gives every dot its distance from r in links and looks at each link at most twice; each dot enters the queue once and its links are looked at when it leaves, so the looks are Σ d(u) = 2|E|, charged to the links and not to the moves","w":["It gives every dot its distance and looks at each link once for each dot; the cost is |V| · |E|","It finds the longest path from r; the cost is |V|², since each dot looks at every other","It gives distances only in trees; the cost is 2|E| because every dot has two links"],"why":"Counting by charges is the amortized cost."},
 {"u":"42k2.1","q":"What does the nearest-first technique say? Which line of its proof needs every length to be at least 0, and what goes wrong without it?","a":"If every length is at least 0, the tentative distance of a dot, when it is settled, is its distance from r; the line that the length of a path is at least dist(u) + ℓ(u, x) needs the rest of the path to have length at least 0; with r to x of length 2, r to y of 3 and y to x of −2, x is settled at 2 and the path through y has length 1","w":["The first dot settled is the farthest; no line needs lengths above 0; nothing goes wrong with negative lengths","Tentative distances are right for every dot at every moment; the first line needs it; with a negative length the recipe never stops","If every length is at least 0 the settled distance is right; the last line needs it; with a negative length every dot is settled at 0"],"why":"Seattle to Portland: min(105 + 185, 230 + 300) = 290."},
 {"u":"42k3.1","q":"What does the cheapest-across technique say? Why does the cycle in T + e cross the split a second time, and why is T + e − f a spanning tree?","a":"For every split, a cheapest link crossing it belongs to some cheapest spanning tree; a closed walk crosses a split an even number of times, so the cycle through e crosses again along a link f of T; T + e − f is in one piece with n − 1 links, a spanning tree by the any-two-of-three technique","w":["The cheapest link of all is in every spanning tree; the cycle crosses once; T + e − f has n links","For every split the dearest crossing link is in no tree; the cycle crosses three times; T + e − f is a tree because f is an end","For every split a cheapest crossing link is in some cheapest tree; the cycle crosses again because T is a path; T + e − f is a tree because it has no dots off the cycle"],"why":"cost(e) ≤ cost(f): the exchange is why greed is safe here."},
 {"u":"42k4.1","q":"What does the doubling-piece technique say? Why does a dot’s piece at least double each time the dot is relabelled?","a":"Relabelling the smaller piece at each merge relabels each dot at most log₂ n times, so all the merges together cost at most n log₂ n relabellings; a dot is relabelled only when its piece joins one at least as large, so after j relabellings its piece has at least 2ʲ dots","w":["Each merge relabels n/2 dots, so n merges cost n²/2; a piece doubles because two dots join","Each dot is relabelled once; the pieces double because the links are sorted","Relabelling the larger piece costs n log₂ n; a dot’s piece halves at each relabelling"],"why":"Charged to the dots, not to the merges."},
 {"u":"42k5.1","q":"Why does a negative length break the nearest-first technique? Why is n²/2 not the cost of n merges by the doubling-piece technique?","a":"With a length −2, x is settled at 2 and a path of length 1 reaches it later; and a dot is relabelled at most log₂ n times, so the costly merges are rare","w":["Negative lengths make the queue empty; and n merges do cost n²/2","A negative length is settled first; and each merge relabels one dot","Negative lengths are not numbers; and n²/2 is below n log₂ n"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42l1.1","q":"What does the best-pieces technique say? Why does a one-way graph with no cycle always have a dot with no link coming in?","a":"If a path from r to w is shortest, each of its pieces, from a dot u on it to a later dot x on it, is a shortest path from u to x; walking backwards along links from any dot must stop, or it would repeat a dot and close a cycle, and where it stops no link comes in","w":["Every piece of any path is shortest; a graph with no cycle has no links, so no link comes in anywhere","If a path is shortest, its first link is the shortest link; some dot has no link in because the graph is in two pieces","If a path is shortest its pieces are shortest only when lengths are above 0; a dot with no link in exists because r is one by choice"],"why":"Put that dot first, take it away, and go on: the forward order."},
 {"u":"42l2.1","q":"What does the rounds technique say? Why do n − 1 rounds suffice when no cycle has negative total, and how does trading in a circle become a cycle of negative total?","a":"dₖ(w) is the least of dₖ₋₁(w) and dₖ₋₁(u) + ℓ(u, w) over the links (u, w); with no negative cycle dₙ₋₁ is the distance, and some cycle has negative total exactly when a further round still lowers some value; a shortest walk is then a path, of at most n − 1 links; with ℓ = −ln(rate), a round of trades whose rates multiply to more than 1, as 1.36 · 0.68 · 1.09 ≈ 1.008, has total below 0","w":["dₖ is the distance after k settled dots; n − 1 rounds suffice because there are n − 1 links; a circle of trades has positive total, since money grows","dₖ(w) is the least of dₖ₋₁(u) + ℓ(u, w); n rounds are always needed; trades become a negative cycle by taking ℓ = rate − 1","The rounds technique needs every length at least 0; one round suffices; a circle of trades has total 0 by fairness"],"why":"The cost is about n|E|, with one more round to test."},
 {"u":"42l3.1","q":"What does the stops-allowed technique say? Why does a best path with stops among 1 to k pass dot k at most once?","a":"Dₖ(i, j) is the least of Dₖ₋₁(i, j) and Dₖ₋₁(i, k) + Dₖ₋₁(k, j), so with no negative cycle Dₙ gives every distance after n³ steps; a path meets no dot twice, so it either avoids k or passes it once, and its two pieces, before and after k, have stops among 1 to k − 1","w":["Dₖ(i, j) is the least length of a path with at most k links; a best path passes k once because k is the largest name","Dₖ(i, j) = Dₖ₋₁(i, j) + Dₖ₋₁(k, k); a best path may pass k many times when lengths are negative","Dₖ gives distances after n² steps; a best path never passes k, since stops are among 1 to k − 1"],"why":"In the worked graph D₂(1, 3) = min(5, 4 − 2) = 2."},
 {"u":"42l4.1","q":"What does the alignment-route technique say? What do the three kinds of link in the grid stand for, and why do they all go forward?","a":"The best alignment of two strings is a longest route through their grid, and 39o’s table is the forward pass of the forward-order technique; a link right is a letter of the second string against a gap, a link down a letter of the first against a gap, and a link down and right two letters against each other; each uses a letter, so every link goes from an earlier place to a later one in the order row by row","w":["The best alignment is a shortest route; the links stand for match, mismatch and stop; they go forward because scores are positive","The best alignment is a spanning tree of the grid; the three links are the three letters; they go both ways","The best alignment is a longest route; links right and down are matches and the slanted link a gap; they go forward because the grid is square"],"why":"A/A, C/−, G/G, T/T scores 1 − 1 + 1 + 1 = 2."},
 {"u":"42l5.1","q":"Why does adding the same amount to every length not let the nearest first handle negative lengths? Why do the pieces of a longest path in a graph with cycles not have to be longest?","a":"A path with more links gains more, so the best path can change; and in a graph with cycles a longest path must avoid repeats, so a longer piece that meets the rest of the path cannot be put in","w":["Adding the same amount keeps every best path; and the pieces of a longest path are always longest","The amount would have to be negative; and longest paths do not exist in graphs with cycles","Adding changes the dots, not the lengths; and pieces are longest only in crowded graphs"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42m1.1","q":"What does the two-stores technique say? Why are neighbour lists the right store for a road map?","a":"The grid takes n² entries and answers “are u and w joined?” in one look; the neighbour lists take n + 2|E| entries, answer it in at most d(u) looks, and give the links at u in d(u) looks where the grid needs n; a road map is thin: with n ≈ 2.4 · 10⁷ and |E| ≈ 5.8 · 10⁷ the grid has about 5.7 · 10¹⁴ entries, almost all 0, and the lists about 8.2 · 10⁷","w":["The grid takes n entries and the lists n²; lists suit a road map because roads are long","Both stores take n + 2|E| entries; the grid suits a road map because it answers in one look","The lists take n² entries and answer in one look; a road map is crowded, so lists are needed"],"why":"The lists are about 7 million times smaller."},
 {"u":"42m2.1","q":"What does the heap-run technique say? Why does pushing fresh entries, and skipping stale ones, cost at most a factor of two?","a":"With neighbour lists and a heap, the nearest first costs about (n + |E|) log₂ n moves; there is at most one fresh entry per lowering, so at most |E| + 1 in the heap, and log₂(|E| + 1) ≤ log₂ n² = 2 log₂ n; a stale entry is taken out once and skipped","w":["The nearest first costs n² with a heap; stale entries double the heap each time","With a heap the cost is n + |E|, with no log; stale entries are never pushed","The cost is (n + |E|) log₂ n; stale entries cost a factor of n, since each dot has n of them"],"why":"On a crowded graph a plain list costs n², below the heap’s n² log₂ n."},
 {"u":"42m3.1","q":"What does the sorting-inside technique say? How does the star turn listing by distance into sorting?","a":"A recipe that lists the dots in order of distance from r, and learns about lengths only by comparing sums of them, makes at least log₂((n − 1)!) comparisons on some graph with n dots; in a star, r is joined to w₁, …, wₙ₋₁ with different lengths, the distance of wᵢ is ℓᵢ, so listing the wᵢ by distance lists the ℓᵢ in order","w":["Every recipe for distances makes n² comparisons; the star has n² links to sort","A recipe that compares makes at most log₂ n comparisons; the star needs no sorting, since all its lengths are equal","A recipe that does arithmetic makes at least n log₂ n steps; the star turns sorting into finding a longest path"],"why":"About n log₂ n, by the answer-count technique (22l, 39l)."},
 {"u":"42m4.1","q":"What does the seen-set technique say? Why must the walk keep a table of seen positions, and what does it cost in room?","a":"Walking out in layers over a graph made by a technique, with the seen dots kept in a scrambled table, costs on average a fixed number of looks for each link followed, and room for every dot seen; the graph is not stored, so only the table says whether a neighbour made by the technique is new and should enter the queue; the room is one entry for each position seen, 181 440 for the eight tiles","w":["It stores the whole graph first; no table is needed; the room is 9! links","It walks without remembering; a position may be visited many times at no cost; the room is one position","It keeps the seen dots in a heap; the table is to sort the positions; the room is log₂ of the positions"],"why":"The farthest positions of the eight tiles are 31 moves away."},
 {"u":"42m5.1","q":"Why is the heap not the best store for the nearest first on a crowded graph? Why does the floor of the sorting-inside technique not bind every recipe?","a":"On a crowded graph a plain list costs n², below the heap’s n² log₂ n; and the floor binds recipes that learn only by comparing: arithmetic on whole-number lengths can do better","w":["The heap is always best; and the floor binds every recipe","A crowded graph has no heap; and the floor is only for trees","The heap costs n³ on any graph; and the floor fails because sorting is fast"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42n"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42n"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42n1.1","q":"What is a slack, and what is a dictionary? What does the slack-form technique say, and why does a dictionary hold for every plan?","a":"The slack of a limit aᵢ · x ≤ bᵢ is sᵢ = bᵢ − aᵢ · x; a dictionary is the list of equations giving the kept unknowns and the value z in terms of the unknowns on the right; a dictionary whose constants are all at least 0 names a corner, by setting every unknown on the right to 0; it holds for every plan, allowed or not, since it is the same equations rearranged","w":["A slack is an unused unknown; a dictionary is a list of corners; a dictionary names a corner only when its constants are 0; it holds for allowed plans only","A slack is bᵢ + aᵢ · x; a dictionary is the grid A; every dictionary names the best corner; it holds because slacks are at least 0","A slack is the value z; a dictionary lists the prices; the slack-form technique says the corner is best; it holds for the corner alone"],"why":"The glass works starts at the corner (0, 0) with z = 0 + 3x + 5y."},
 {"u":"42n2.1","q":"What does the pivot technique say, and what is the ratio test for? In the glass works, why did s₃ leave in the second step and not s₁?","a":"If an unknown on the right of z has a positive coefficient, let it rise from 0 until the first kept unknown reaches 0 and swap those two; the new constants are all at least 0 and z has not fallen; the ratio test finds that first kept unknown: the least constant divided by its rate of fall; as x rises, s₁ reaches 0 at x = 4 and s₃ at x = 2, ratios 4/1 and 6/3, so s₃ is first","w":["Swap any two unknowns; the ratio test picks the largest ratio; s₃ left because 6 is larger than 4","Let the unknown with a negative coefficient rise; the ratio test finds the value of z; s₁ left in the second step","Let an unknown rise until z stops growing; the ratio test compares coefficients of z; s₃ left because it had the smallest name"],"why":"The walk ends at z = 36 − (3/2)s₂ − s₃, the corner (2, 6)."},
 {"u":"42n3.1","q":"What does the last-row technique say? Why may the coefficients of x in the two sides of its second line be set equal?","a":"When the walk stops, with z = z* − Σ rⱼxⱼ − Σ yᵢsᵢ and every r and y at least 0, the y’s are covering prices with b · y = z*, so the corner is best, by the price-bound technique; the last dictionary holds for every plan x, and two such expressions equal for every x have the same coefficients and the same constants","w":["The last row gives the best plan; the coefficients are equal because x is 0 at the corner","The y’s are the amounts made; the coefficients may be set equal only at allowed plans","When the walk stops the r’s are the prices; the coefficients are equal by the ratio test"],"why":"The glass works ends with the prices (0, 3/2, 1) of 42g2."},
 {"u":"42n4.1","q":"What does the first-phase technique say? Why can a step leave z where it was, and what does the technique of smallest names do about it?","a":"If the dictionary at x = 0 has a negative constant, add one unknown x₀ ≥ 0 to every limit as aᵢ · x − x₀ ≤ bᵢ and maximise −x₀; the program has an allowed plan exactly when the best value is 0, and the last dictionary of that walk starts the real one; a step leaves z unchanged when the leaving unknown’s constant is already 0, a corner where more than n limits are tight; choosing the smallest name to enter and to leave never circles, held in trust from 1977","w":["Start from any corner; a step never leaves z unchanged; the smallest-name technique makes the walk faster","Add x₀ to z and minimise; z stays put when the entering coefficient is 0; the technique of smallest names stops the walk at once","If a constant is negative the program has no allowed plan; z stays put at every step; the smallest-name technique is proved on this step"],"why":"With that technique the dictionaries never repeat, and there are finitely many."},
 {"u":"42n5.1","q":"Why must the slack with the least ratio leave? What does it mean when no slack falls as the entering unknown rises?","a":"A larger ratio would drive another slack below 0, out of the allowed plans; and when no slack falls the entering unknown can rise for ever: the value is unbounded","w":["The largest ratio must leave, to move farthest; and no slack falling means no allowed plan","Any slack may leave; and no slack falling means the corner is best","The least ratio leaves because it has the smallest name; and no slack falling means z is 0"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["42·cb19"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["42·cb19"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"42·cb19.1.1","q":"What are the class and the keepers of x? Why do the moves carrying x to one place y form one copy of the keepers?","a":"The class of x is the set of all gx, the places the moves carry it to; its keepers are the moves g with gx = x, a part-group; gx = hx exactly when h⁻¹g keeps x, that is, when g is in the copy hK_x; so with y = hx the moves carrying x to y are exactly that one copy","w":["The class is the set of moves fixing x and the keepers the places it reaches; the moves to y are all of G","The class of x is the set of all gx; the keepers are the moves with gx = x; the moves carrying x to y form a part-group of their own","The class is the whole set X; the keepers are the moves that keep everything; the moves to y are one move"],"why":"|G| = |class of x| · |K_x|, so the size of a class divides the size of the group."},
 {"u":"42·cb19.2.1","q":"What does the average-kept technique say? Why does each class add exactly |G| to the count of pairs?","a":"The number of classes is the average, over the moves of G, of the number of things each move keeps; sorted by the thing, the pairs number Σ |K_x| = Σ |G|/|class of x|, and a class C has |C| members each giving |G|/|C|","w":["The number of classes is the number of things the identity keeps; each class adds |C| to the count","The number of classes is |X| divided by |G|; each class adds 1","The number of classes is the average size of a class; each class adds |G|²"],"why":"One set of pairs counted two ways, as in the ends-count technique."},
 {"u":"42·cb19.3.1","q":"Why does a turn by k places keep 2^gcd(6, k) strings? How are the 14 necklaces up to turns and the 13 up to turns and flips found?","a":"The turn splits the six places into gcd(6, k) rings, each of which must be one colour; the six turns keep 64, 2, 4, 8, 4, 2 strings, and the average is 84/6 = 14; three flips through two beads keep 2⁴ and three through two gaps keep 2³, so (84 + 48 + 24)/12 = 13","w":["A turn by k keeps 2ᵏ strings; 64/6 rounds to 14, and 64/12 rounds to 13","A turn by k moves k beads; the 14 come from 2⁶ − 50; the flips remove one more","Every turn keeps 2 strings; 14 = 2 + 12, and 13 = 14 − 1 by symmetry"],"why":"Exactly one pair of the 14 are mirror images that no turn joins."},
 {"u":"42·cb19.4.1","q":"When does a shuffle of the dots keep a graph? Why does a three-ring of the dots keep exactly 4 graphs?","a":"When it carries every kept link to a kept link: when the graph is a union of the rings the shuffle makes on the six possible links; a three-ring makes two rings of three links, and each ring is kept whole or left out: 2² = 4","w":["When it moves no dot; a three-ring keeps 4 graphs because one dot stays","When the graph has no links; a three-ring keeps 2³ graphs, one for each dot","When it carries links to non-links; a three-ring keeps 4 graphs because 6 − 2 = 4"],"why":"(64 + 96 + 48 + 32 + 24)/24 = 11 shapes of graph on four dots."},
 {"u":"42·cb19.5.1","q":"Why is dividing 64 by 6 not the count of necklaces? Why are the keepers of gx not always the keepers of x?","a":"64/6 is not a whole number: classes differ in size, so dividing by |G| is wrong; and the keepers of gx are g K_x g⁻¹, the same size, not always the same set","w":["64/6 is the count, rounded down; and the keepers of gx are always those of x","The count is 64 − 6; and gx has no keepers","Dividing works when 6 is prime; and the keepers of gx are a larger group"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
